/* Cigar Cafe — 世界編の英語本文 4/6（投資・用語大全）。英語版専用。元: data/world*.js */
(function () {
  if (typeof WORLD_DATA === "undefined") return;
  var W = WORLD_DATA;

  W.invest.thesis = "【Why cigars can become assets】Cigars are said to qualify as objects of investment and collecting because three properties coincide: (1) scarcity, (2) quality that improves with ageing, and (3) continual price rises. Cuban Habanos in particular are produced in limited quantities, and limited editions and discontinued lines are finite resources that will never be made again — the foundation that supports their prices.\n【What the scarcity consists of】The once-yearly Edición Limitada (EL) and the country-specific Edición Regional are produced in small numbers, and the Regionals' low availability — 'you cannot buy them without going to that country' — pushes up their secondary-market value. Discontinued Cuban-era Davidoffs and Dunhills and pre-embargo cigars are treated as icons because they 'will never be made again'.\n【The premium of ageing】Rested for years or decades in a proper environment, a cigar loses its rough edges, creating a structure close to wine or aged whisky in which 'time creates value'. The 'marrying' by which the freshly rolled leaves settle into one another progresses, with a sense of unity said to emerge after about 90 days, and long ageing raises both the pleasure value and the asset value. It should be borne in mind, however, that not every cigar improves with age: the fuller-bodied and more powerful the marque to begin with, the greater the headroom is said to be.\n【The tailwind of price rises】Habanos S.A. has raised prices almost every year of late, and in 2022 Cohiba and Trinidad were raised two- to threefold to align with Hong Kong market pricing. That said, things have recently settled down, and the average revision for 2026 is reported to have come in at roughly 3.8% — within single digits.\n【The two faces of price rises】Rising list prices lift the paper gains on existing stock while also raising the cost of new purchases. The recent single-digit revisions, compared with the period when double-digit jumps were the norm, also mean the appeal of banking on future appreciation has faded somewhat; the tailwind should not be assumed to last for ever.\n【Risks not to overlook】(1) Storage failure — mould, dryness or tobacco beetles can render an expensive box worthless in an instant. (2) Counterfeits — forgery is rife among high-priced Habanos. (3) Poor liquidity — as an indulgence with a limited pool of buyers, cigars cannot be turned into cash at once. (4) Legal regulation, customs duties and import restrictions.\n【A market short of data】Unlike wine, cigars have almost no systematic price databases or trackers, making fair value hard to establish. This makes liquidity even harder to assess, and some point out that for casual buyers the clear upside is limited.\n【Counterfeiting as a structural risk】Forgery is rife among high-priced Habanos, and sophisticated fakes imitate even the seals and bands. Since a mistake over authenticity can wipe out the entire investment, confining purchases to official channels with solid provenance is the line of defence that comes before any thought of returns.\n【The hurdle of the time horizon】To capture genuine appreciation requires the patience to rest cigars for ten years or more in a properly managed cellar. There is a sober view that for anyone other than a true collector able to do this, the already high prices leave little room for dramatic gains. This is not a product on which to count for short-term resale profit.\n【Comparison with other tangible assets】Like rare wine and aged whisky, cigars fall into the class of 'tangible, indulgence-based assets' whose supply is finite and whose quality rises with the years. But whereas wine has mature price data and a resale market, cigars have neither in any developed form, and they compare a step less favourably in transparency and convertibility into cash.\n【The non-Cuban option】Cuban cigars are the mainstay of investment, but rare Dominicans such as Opus X and commemorative pieces also command prices on the secondary market. Diversifying across origins broadens the palate, though it should be discounted that many such marques lack the established scarcity narrative of Cuban cigars.\n【A neutral framing】Financial return presupposes 'resting cigars unsmoked and then selling', and losses are entirely possible. The wise view is to treat this as an extension of a hobby and a taste — one asset among a diversified set. The healthy order of priorities is enjoyment first, asset value second. * The prices, multiples and revision rates in this section are all approximate and accounts vary.";

  W.invest.auctions = "【The summit: the humidor auction in Havana】The symbol of cigar auctions is the 'Humidor Charity Auction' held on the final night of the Habanos Festival in Havana each year. One-off pieces — bespoke luxury humidors filled with rare cigars — go under the hammer, and the proceeds are said to be donated to Cuban public health (the medical system).\n【Recent records (approximate; accounts vary)】In 2024 a Cohiba humidor fetched about €4.5 million, setting a new single-lot record at the time, and the night's total was reported at about €17.8–18.9 million. In 2025 the Cohiba Behike 15th-anniversary humidor raised the single-lot record further to about €4.6 million, but the night's total, at about €16.25–16.41 million, is said to have fallen from the previous year.\n【How to read the records】The top single-lot price (one humidor) and the night's total are different things, and the figures vary between reports. Year-on-year totals do not necessarily climb steadily: note that in 2025 a single-lot record and a fall in the total occurred at the same time.\n【The secondary and vintage market】In the secondary market for existing rarities, Britain's C.Gars Ltd. (among the world's largest online Cuban cigar auctions) is the central presence, holding sales since the late 1990s and handling many pre-embargo lots, Cuban-era Davidoffs and Dunhills and rare Cohibas. According to managing director Mitchell Orchant, it is the pre-embargo lots that attract the keenest bidding.\n【Guide prices from actual sales (approximate)】Reported examples include about £8,000–12,000 for a 1988 box of 25 Davidoff Dom Pérignon and about £8,300 (about $11,500) for a 1985 box of 10 Dunhill Estupendos (tubed). Even within the same discontinued vintage, hammer prices diverge widely with age, box completeness and state of preservation.\n【C.Gars as a venue】C.Gars Ltd. began handling cigars in the late 1990s alongside wine and spirits auctions and is now known as a dedicated online Cuban cigar auction house. With sell-through rates exceeding 90% in some sales, it has become the hub of the secondary market, connecting collectors with owners wishing to turn discontinued and pre-embargo stock into cash.\n【The general auction houses】Bonhams and Christie's do not specialise in cigars, but occasionally handle humidors and cigars alongside their wine and spirits sales. The selection is not as deep as at a specialist auction, but famous collections and pieces of distinguished history do reach the market through the general houses, and on the underpinning of provenance they can be trusted. Overall, the highest prices concentrate on limited editions, discontinued lines, pre-embargo pieces and lots with clear provenance.\n【The charity character】The main festival auction in Havana is less a place of pure price discovery than a festive occasion coupled with donations to Cuban healthcare. Hammer prices carry an admixture of publicity and brand prestige, and it should be understood that they do not necessarily reflect the merit of the cigars inside alone.\n【What drives high prices】The common threads of high prices are small limited runs, discontinuation, the pre-embargo era, and clarity of provenance. Add the narrative of an anniversary or the artisanal added value of a bespoke humidor, and prices tend to leap.\n【The appeal of Regionals and ELs】The Edición Regional is allocated to limited countries, and the low availability — obtainable only on the spot — creates secondary value. The once-yearly Edición Limitada is likewise produced in restricted numbers and tends to firm in price to the point of vanishing from the market some time after release.\n【How to approach an auction as a buyer】At auction the basics are to check one's sense of the market against several past hammer prices beforehand, and to ask for condition photographs, factory codes and verification of provenance. The buyer's premium and any customs duties and regulations on import must also be built into the total cost.\n【The limits of the market】As noted, systematic price data are scarce, and hammer prices are heavily swayed by timing, provenance, condition and publicity. Prices are close to one-off valuations, and one should proceed on the premise that even the same marque can split widely in price on condition. * All amounts are approximate and accounts vary.";

  var ME = [
    { name: "Gurkha Royal Courtesan",
      price: "About $1.36 million per cigar (approximate; accounts vary)",
      note: "The piece most frequently cited as 'the world's most expensive cigar'. Made in the Dominican Republic/Honduras, it is said to use rare Himalayan-grown tobacco tended with Fiji Water, and is known for its lore: a wrapper finished in 24-carat gold leaf, a band set with a diamond of about 5 carats, and infusion with Louis XIII cognac (Rémy Martin Black Pearl). Rollers are even said to work blindfolded. It is not, however, a marque that circulates in quantity through normal commerce; it is best seen as the ultimate PR exercise, staging its price through ornament and story, and the $1.36 million figure itself is disputed. A textbook case of why 'the merit of the cigar itself' and 'added value and staging' should be judged separately." },
    { name: "Gran Habano #5 El Gigante",
      price: "About $185,000 (sold January 2013)",
      note: "One of the world's largest cigars, about 19 feet (about 5.8 m) long and about 3 feet wide, said to weigh about 1,600 pounds and to use more than 16,000 wrapper leaves. Made by Juan Panesso, who runs CigarsDirect.com, this one-off piece was actually sold in January 2013 to a private collector and enthusiast for $185,000 — about $15,000 below the asking price. Where the Gurkha above aims at a 'masterpiece for smoking', here the source of value is the record itself — 'the world's largest' — a good example of a different basis for the price." },
    { name: "Regius Double Corona",
      price: "About $54,000 per cigar (2013)",
      note: "Sold in 2013 by the British brand Regius to the businessman Callum Jones. The price covered not just the cigar itself but 1,000 custom cigars of a dedicated blend, a tour of the Nicaraguan factory and the right to put his own name on a new line — an 'experiential' price rather than payment for a single stick. A good example of how many of the 'most expensive cigars' are really bundles of experience, perks and ornament, and of why the headline figure should not be taken at face value." },
    { name: "Gurkha His Majesty's Reserve",
      price: "Around $750 per cigar",
      note: "Said to be produced in extremely small numbers — under 75 boxes a year — and known for its infusion with cognac (Louis XIII). A byword for hard-to-obtain top-end brands and the staple marque of Gurkha's 'high-price theatre' line. Less extreme than the Royal Courtesan above, and sitting in a range one can realistically aim to buy and smoke, it is the most attainable of the staged offerings. Prices vary with availability." },
    { name: "King of Denmark",
      price: "About $4,500 per cigar",
      note: "A Danish ultra-luxury marque and a fixture on most-expensive lists. It is said to offer complete personalisation — the owner's name engraved in gold leaf, adornment with precious metals and diamonds — and is often spoken of together with its dedicated humidor (about $8,500) in sterling silver, 24-carat gold plate and Swarovski crystal. Here too the bulk of the price is the added value of ornament and personalisation rather than payment for the smoke itself — which it shares with the staged marques above." },
    { name: "Cohiba Behike BHK",
      price: "Around $450 per cigar (the peak of regular production)",
      note: "The peak of regular-production Habanos. It uses the rare medio tiempo (leaves from the very top of the plant, obtainable only in tiny numbers) and holds a high, stable price in the market. With a high list price and demand that readily tightens, it is the exemplar of a regular-production marque with strong asset character. The festival humidor versions are in a class entirely apart: the 2025 Behike 15th-anniversary humidor is said to have set the single-lot record of about €4.6 million, symbolising the price chasm that lies between a production cigar and a one-off piece." },
    { name: "Arturo Fuente Opus X 'BBMF' / commemorative pieces",
      price: "Hundreds to thousands of dollars per cigar (varies widely by piece)",
      note: "The representative non-Cuban collector's marque. Opus X was born of the challenge of growing, on the company's own Dominican estate, wrapper leaf that was said to grow nowhere but Cuba, and its scarcity and quality won it a fervent following. With allocations limited, it is hard to obtain even at list price, and large commemorative pieces and limited releases can soar on the secondary market. A good example that established high-price markets exist outside Cuba as well. Prices vary greatly with the individual piece and its condition." }
  ];
  if (W.invest.mostExpensive.length === ME.length) W.invest.mostExpensive.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, ME[i]); });

  var VL = [
    { name: "Davidoff Dom Pérignon (Cuban era)",
      desc: "The icon of Cuban-made Davidoff. Launched in the late 1970s and discontinued around 1989–1991. Existing only in the Cuban era — it never moved to the Dominican Republic — it is an object of desire, with examples now trading at upwards of $550 a cigar. A 1988 box of 25 is reckoned at about £8,000–12,000 (about $11,000–16,500), and early-1980s boxes at about £4,000–5,000 as a guide. Cuban-made Davidoff is said to have withdrawn in 1991 with the intention that stock be disposed of by the end of 1992, and that scarcity underpins the price. Even within the same marque, prices vary greatly with age and condition, and verifying provenance is indispensable." },
    { name: "Davidoff Château series",
      desc: "The popular Cuban-era Davidoff line named after the great Bordeaux wine estates. Davidoff was manufactured by Cubatabaco, the Cuban monopoly, but withdrew in 1991 — against a background said to include the Cuban side's dislike of the rivalry with Dunhill — and the whole line ceased production, soaring in value as a discontinued legend. Stock was reportedly to be disposed of by the end of 1992, and sound examples surviving through legitimate channels are extremely few. Naming that appeals to wine lovers as well helped its popularity along, and it is treated, alongside the Dom Pérignon, as an icon of Cuban-era Davidoff." },
    { name: "Dunhill (Cuban-made)",
      desc: "Rolled in Cuba from 1982 to 1991, then moved to the Dominican Republic. Cuban-made examples from the 1980s are said to have scored a perfect 100 in Cigar Aficionado more than once and are ranked 'among the greatest cigars ever made'. Behind the discontinuation, it is said, lay the fact that the monopoly Cubatabaco was carrying two premium private labels at once — Davidoff and Dunhill — and did not want them competing. One assessment holds that 'interest in fine vintage Dunhills of sound provenance grows year by year, and they are consistently more sought-after than Davidoff', and in recent years they have been rated above Davidoff. A 1985 box of 10 Estupendos fetched about £8,300 at auction." },
    { name: "Pre-embargo Cuban cigars",
      desc: "Cigars legitimately imported from Cuba before President Kennedy signed the embargo on 3 February 1962. The ultimate vintage, aged for more than half a century, they are said to be the lots that attract the keenest bidding at C.Gars auctions. In sound condition they can reach several times their original price, but pieces this old vary widely in preservation, and proof of authenticity and provenance decisively sways the price. The very history of being barred from legal circulation in the United States by the embargo is itself the source of their scarcity and their story." },
    { name: "Trinidad Fundadores (diplomatic)",
      desc: "A legendary marque said to have been made from around 1980 for Fidel Castro, leaving the country only as diplomatic gifts. Public sale began in 1998, first in Cuba and then in Mexico and Canada. The retail Fundadores measures 7 1/2 × 40, slightly thicker than the diplomatic version (ring gauge 38), so the two are held not to be entirely identical. Note, however, that Castro himself said he gave only Cohiba as diplomatic gifts, so accounts of the 'diplomatic-only' story vary. The more a piece claims early or diplomatic origin, the more important the verification of provenance." },
    { name: "Reserva / Gran Reserva",
      desc: "The top lines, rolled from leaf of particularly fine harvest years, selected and long-aged before rolling. The guide is leaf aged a further 3 years for a Reserva and 5 or more for a Gran Reserva; production is limited, and the box carries the harvest year (vintage) and an individual serial number, so their origin is explicit and their collection value is held to be the highest. A representative case of asset value underwritten by 'traceability' — a year on the box, the counterpart of a wine vintage — with pieces from great years especially highly rated." },
    { name: "Cohiba 1966 (50th anniversary)",
      desc: "Released in 2016 to mark the 50th anniversary of Cohiba's founding. Only 1,966 of the dedicated humidors were made — a symbolic limited number that writes the founding year straight into the production run — and the combination of commemorative character and tiny production gives it high collector value. A typical example of secondary-market prices supported by the 'story' of a brand anniversary and the scarcity of an explicit limited number; such commemorative pieces not uncommonly exceed list price straight after release." },
    { name: "Cohiba / Partagás commemorative series",
      desc: "Limited commemorative series in wooden boxes marking brand anniversaries generally. Made with specially selected aged leaf and ornamentation, they readily become hard-to-find prizes once sold out. The more a release combines publicity, a limited number and ageing potential — Partagás founding anniversaries, Cohiba's various anniversaries and so on — the more readily it is said to command a price. Because anniversary releases have their timing and quantities fixed in advance, their market is comparatively easy to read, which also makes them a suitable entry point for a beginner learning how 'limited editions with a story' move in price. Popular marques, though, are prime targets for counterfeiting as well, and provenance checks are essential." }
  ];
  if (W.invest.vintageLegends.length === VL.length) W.invest.vintageLegends.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, VL[i]); });

  W.invest.dating = "【The basics of dating a box】On the base of a Cuban cigar box are ink-stamped a three-letter code identifying the factory and a date code showing the month and year of boxing. As the starting point for authentication and dating, this is the first information an investor should check. Factory codes are changed frequently — with several in use at once — as an anti-counterfeiting measure, so checking against a reference table is considered the reliable approach.\n【The three date-code systems】(1) The 'NIVELACUSO' system of about 1985–1998 — a cipher in which each of the ten letters stands for a digit. (2) The 'NETAGIDOCU' system of about 1998–1999 — the same principle with the letter string changed. (3) The current system from 2000 — the first three letters of the Spanish month plus the last two digits of the year (ENE = January … DIC = December; e.g. 'AGO 19' = boxed August 2019), standardised for easy reading.\n【'REVISADO' on 2000 boxes】Following the quality problems of the 1999 tobacco crop, there are cases where boxes from 2000 that passed quality control carry a 'REVISADO' (inspected) stamp. It serves as supporting evidence for dating, so it should not be overlooked on boxes of that year.\n【Factory codes are a moving target】Since 1998, factory codes have been reassigned frequently by algorithm as an anti-counterfeiting measure, and the same factory may hold several codes at once. The experts who can decode them are few, so the premise is the latest reference tables and cross-checking of several sources. One should never judge on the code alone, but weigh it together with the seals, the stamps and the quality of the leaf.\n【Storage conditions for investment】The guide for long ageing is put at around 16–18°C and 65–70% relative humidity, with the market speaking of a range of 65–72%. For long ageing many collectors prefer the slightly lower 65–68%. Management in a Spanish-cedar-lined humidor or a converted wine cabinet is the standard.\n【Tobacco-beetle precautions】Tobacco beetle eggs are said to hatch more readily above about 72°F (about 22°C) and 72% RH; held at 16–18°C and 65–70%, hatching is biologically unlikely. 'Humidity below 72%, and the temperature not allowed to climb' is the key to protecting expensive stock. One outbreak can riddle a whole box with holes and mean a total loss, so be especially vigilant as temperatures rise with the seasons.\n【Temperature swings and condensation】Sudden temperature differences invite condensation, cracked wrappers and loss of aroma. Ideally, keep boxes away from direct sunlight, heating and windows, and maintain a gently stable environment through the year. For long ageing, 'stable numbers' are held to matter more to preserving value than 'high numbers'.\n【Cabinets versus dress boxes】For investment and ageing, the 'cabinet' — cigars bundled and packed in a slide-lid box of Spanish cedar — is held to be favourable: the explanation given is that air is held between the cigars, and the ageing proceeds collectively as the cedar aroma is imparted. Packed more loosely than a dress box, they are widely thought to improve better with the years.\n【Why cedar】Spanish cedar holds humidity in moderation, is said to act against insects, and imparts its aroma while advancing the ageing gently. This is why cedar lines humidors and cabinets, and it is the standard vessel for investment stock as well.\n【The mechanism of ageing】Within the box, the oleoresins (resinous matter) and moisture of the leaves migrate between them and spread evenly, taking off the rough edges and pulling the flavour together. This is the process called 'marrying', said to run at least 90 days and, over the long term, for years.\n【The timeline at the raw-material stage】Most Cuban cigars are in any case rolled only after 25–50 days of curing, 25–90 days of fermentation and a further six months to two years of ageing after harvest. The whole process runs some 18–24 months, and ageing after purchase is its continuation.\n【Measuring and holding humidity】For expensive stock, stabilise the numbers with a reliable hygrometer (calibrated if possible) and conditioning media such as beads, gel or Boveda packs. Sudden humidity swings invite cracked wrappers and loss of aroma, so mind the frequency of opening and the temperature differences at the storage site.\n【Condition equals asset value】Keeping boxes unopened, with warranty seal, stamps and labels intact, is the key to preserving asset value. Proper management in a humidor, and the avoidance of water damage, fading and insect damage, will weigh heavily in the valuation at a future sale. * Figures are approximate and accounts vary.";

  W.invest.start = "【What to collect first】For appreciation, the focus falls on (1) the Edición Limitada (EL), (2) the Edición Regional, (3) Reservas and Gran Reservas of good years, (4) Cohiba Behike and the anniversary marques, and (5) discontinued legends such as Cuban-era Davidoff and Dunhill and pre-embargo cigars.\n【A middle way that enjoys the ageing too】To take both asset value and pleasure, the standard method is to buy full-bodied regular-production cigars of good ageing potential 'by the box', resting half and tasting half. You can follow the marrying (the process by which the leaves settle into one another) on your own palate, and train your eye for good years and good factories. The tasting notes also become data for reading the ageing curve of the pieces you keep.\n【Facing the counterfeit risk】The more expensive the Habano, the more sophisticated the forgeries in circulation, so the iron rule is to buy only through official channels and assured provenance. Bargains far below the market, glass-topped boxes and inconsistent codes should be doubted at once. Treating the cost and effort of authentication as a necessary expense of investing is what averts losses.\n【The authentication procedure】Habanos has introduced a self-destructing new warranty seal. The decisive checks are said to be holding the hologram on the right (two-colour 2D/3D lettering) up to the light to see it change in relief, and verifying the unique barcode on the left at verificacion.habanos.com. Fakes are often flat in their printing and lack the hologram's depth.\n【The red flags】(1) Uneven colour, blemishes or a rough feel to the wrapper. (2) Inconsistent factory codes — if, say, the factory code on the base of a Cohiba box does not square with the official reference tables, be suspicious (Cohiba is said to be confined to particular codes). (3) No genuine Cuban product exists in glass-topped or clear plastic boxes. (4) Pieces extremely far below the market. All are signs to doubt at once.\n【Record-keeping and provenance】Keep a ledger or app recording purchase date, seller, price, factory code, storage conditions and smoking notes, and save pre-opening condition photographs and receipts. This becomes the proof of provenance at a future sale, and clarity of provenance feeds directly into the hammer price. High-resolution photographs of the base code, the seals and the whole box will also help with any later authenticity enquiry.\n【Choosing the box format】For investment and ageing, the 'cabinet (SLB)' — cigars bundled with a ribbon and packed in a Spanish cedar box — is held to be favourable, and it can be chosen for many Cuban marques. Packed the more loosely, they are said to improve the better with the years — a format worth choosing deliberately for long storage.\n【Thinking about budget allocation】On a limited budget, rather than chasing a single much-talked-of ultra-expensive cigar, it is held more realistic to assemble a few boxes of good ageing potential and then invest in the storage equipment. Investing first in the vessel (humidor or cellar) carries the most certain return of all, in the sense of protecting the stock.\n【Keep the exit (sale) in view】At the moment of buying, imagine the moment of selling, and have provenance, condition and accessories in order from the start. Valuations rise the more complete the lot — full box, unopened, seals intact, booklet and sealing papers present; conversely, split lots, missing boxes and unknown provenance depreciate heavily. Sale routes include specialist auctions such as C.Gars, buy-back by official retailers and collector-to-collector deals, all of which involve fees and time that should be built into the plan.\n【How to begin】Buying pieces of clear provenance from trusted official retailers or auctions with a track record is the greatest defence. The path least likely to fail is to go deep on one or two brands first, start with a few good boxes, and invest up front in storage equipment. Make it your watchword that a full, unopened box with all its accessories sells highest. * This section describes general tendencies; the wise course is to invest at your own risk, as an extension of the pleasure.";

  var LEX = [
  "The single outermost leaf that clothes the cigar. The Spanish capa means 'cloak' or 'mantle'. It contributes greatly to appearance, aroma and flavour, and is held to be the finest and most expensive leaf selected from the plant. Its shades are graded in steps from claro to oscuro, and its character is set by the cultivation method (shade or sun) and the variety. A smooth, lustrous leaf is considered the mark of quality.",
  "The intermediate leaf that gathers the filler into a cylinder. The Spanish capote likewise derives from 'cloak'. An unsung workhorse responsible for holding the shape and for even combustion, it is not required to match the wrapper's looks, but sturdy leaves with good elasticity and burning qualities are chosen. Leaves that fell short of wrapper standard are sometimes assigned to this role.",
  "The blend of several kinds of leaf that forms the cigar's core. The Spanish tripa means 'guts' or 'entrails', denoting the cigar's insides. Leaves of differing strength and combustibility — ligero, viso, seco, volado — are combined to set the heart of the taste. The art of the blend creates a marque's character, and leaves of different origins are often combined.",
  "Premium filler using whole leaves folded lengthways. Larga means 'long'. It gives even combustion, complex flavour and a straight ash, and is held to be a condition of the premium handmade cigar. The inscription 'Totalmente a mano' (totally handmade) on a box guarantees hand-rolled long filler.",
  "Filler made of cut fragments of leaf. Corta means 'short'; picadura is 'shredded tobacco'. It puts offcuts from the manufacturing process to use, and is common in inexpensive and machine-made cigars. It burns fast, and its complexity of flavour and firmness of ash are held inferior to long filler.",
  "An intermediate method that wraps a long-filler core in short filler, or combines the two. Mixta means 'mixed'. It aims at a better smoke than a machine-made cigar while containing cost, and is used in mid-priced cigars.",
  "The finish that seals the mouth end with a small round piece of the same leaf as the wrapper. On fine handmade cigars two or three layers are applied. Before smoking, the cut is made on the head side of this cap's rim, and that is where the lips go. Perilla means 'knob' or 'grip'.",
  "A decorative technique that finishes the head by twisting the end of the wrapper up into a flag shape without cutting it away. Also called a pigtail, from the resemblance. Often seen on classic shapes such as the Lancero, it is taken as proof of the roller's craft. It can also be lit without cutting.",
  "The closed end taken into the mouth. Cabeza means 'head'. It carries the cap, which is opened before smoking by straight cut, punch or V-cut.",
  "The open end that is lit. In official Habanos terminology boquilla (literally 'mouthpiece', though it denotes the lighting end); colloquially pie, 'foot'. In some shapes, such as the Perfecto, the foot too is closed.",
  "The cylindrical core made by wrapping the filler in the binder. This is the state before the wrapper goes on: the bunch is placed in a mould (molde) and pressed into shape, and only then is the wrapper rolled. How the bunch is packed (the bunching methods described below) governs draw and burn.",
  "A bunching method in which each filler leaf is rolled into a slender tube before the tubes are laid together and bound. Held to be among the oldest techniques, originating in seventeenth-century Seville, it leaves air channels between the leaves that let the aromas come through well. Being laborious — one roller can produce few cigars a day — it is now rare.",
  "A bunching method in which each filler leaf is folded into pleats, accordion-fashion, and stacked. The folds create air channels and yield a good draw. Easier to master and more productive than entubado, it is widely used for premium cigars.",
  "A bunching method in which the filler leaves are laid flat on one another and rolled up like a book (or a taco). The most common and the easiest to learn, but because the leaves lie directly on one another its airflow is held inferior to the accordion and tube methods.",
  "A semi-mechanical bunching method in which filler and binder are rolled on the Lieberman device, with its rubber mat and channel. It produces consistent, uniform bunches, and is placed between handmade and machine-made.",
  "A two-part hardwood form that clasps the bunch and gives it shape. It usually carries around ten recesses and is tightened in a press (prensa). Moulds are made slightly slimmer than the finished cigar, and the press is loosened at intervals so the cigars can be rotated to keep seam lines from forming where the halves meet.",
  "The screw press in which the stacked moulds are tightened down. It compresses and trues the bunch, fixing the shape before the wrapper is rolled. Tightening, loosening and rotating are repeated to erase the seam lines.",
  "A finish shaped to a squared cross-section rather than a cylinder. It is said to have arisen originally to pack cigars close against one another in the box and stop them rolling. A version with sharper corners is called a square press. Many devotees prefer the difference it makes to mouthfeel and burn.",
  "The paper band printed with the marque's name or crest and wrapped round the body. Anilla means 'small ring'. It is said to have spread in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries to keep the fingers unstained, to certify authenticity and to decorate. Bands are also collected; the hobby of collecting them is called vitolphily.",
  "A second band added below the main one on limited and commemorative editions. It is used to identify special lines such as the Edición Limitada and the Añejados.",
  "The decorative ribbon of paper that seals the edges and joints of a cigar box. Its colours and patterns differ by workshop and brand, and it also serves as a clue in authentication. The artisan who applies it is called a fileteadora.",
  "The skilled artisan who rolls cigars by hand. Torcer means 'to twist, to roll', so the word means 'one who rolls'. The feminine form is torcedora, and tabaquero is used as a synonym. In Cuba, rollers are graded into categories after long training.",
  "An artisan or maker of cigars. Used as a synonym for torcedor, though it can also denote, more broadly, anyone engaged in the cigar industry.",
  "The great room in a factory where the artisans (torcedores) roll by hand at rows of desks. Traditionally a lector (reader) reads newspapers and novels aloud from a raised platform. The factory's in-house name for a shape, the 'vitola de galera', also derives from this word.",
  "The traditional reader who reads newspapers, novels and political essays aloud in the galera, easing the rollers' labour and broadening their minds. The custom began in nineteenth-century Cuba, and marque names such as Montecristo are said to derive from novels read aloud.",
  "The skilled worker who sorts and grades the wrapper leaves by shade, size and quality for each vitola. He performs the vital step of preparing the leaves handed on to the torcedor.",
  "The sorting stage in which the leaves, after curing and fermentation, are classified by shade and quality for wrapper, binder and filler. Escoger means 'to choose'. Wrappers are graded especially finely by colour, which is used to match the shade of the cigars in a single box.",
  "The stage at which the central rib (palillo) of the leaf is removed by hand — and the building where the work is done. For wrappers the leaf is split into two half-leaves. Removing the rib makes the leaf easier to roll and the burn more even.",
  "The in-house name a factory gives each shape and size (e.g. Julieta = Churchill, Laguito No.1 = Lancero). The same vitola de galera carries different retail names across marques.",
  "The retail marque name given for the consumer (e.g. Cohiba Siglo VI). Salida means 'dispatch, market'. Even the same factory designation is sold under different salida names from marque to marque.",
  "A field or farm where tobacco is grown. The finest fields of Cuba's famed Vuelta Abajo are called vega fina and yield the best leaf for wrappers. Celebrated vegas include Don Alejandro and El Corojo.",
  "A farmer or grower of tobacco — one who works a vega. Many families hand the craft of cultivation down the generations, and growing wrapper leaf demands especially high skill.",
  "The farm barn in which the harvested leaves are hung on poles for natural curing and first conditioning. Over about 50 days the chlorophyll in the leaf breaks down and the colour turns from green to brown. Traditionally built long and narrow on an east–west axis to secure ventilation.",
  "The method of growing wrapper leaf in shade under thin cheesecloth. Tapar means 'to cover'. Shielded from direct sun, the leaves grow thin, smooth and fine-veined, suiting them to fine wrappers. Connecticut Shade is the classic example.",
  "Cultivation in direct sunlight. Sol means 'sun'. It produces thick leaves, oily and powerful, suited to filler, dark wrappers and wrappers prone to 'tooth' (fine graininess).",
  "The thick, oil-rich leaves grown at the top of the plant, where they take the most sun. They give the most powerful, concentrated flavour and nicotine. Being thick they burn slowly, and are used sparingly as the core of a slow, long smoke. Ironically, the root word ligero means 'light'.",
  "Leaves from the middle to upper part of the plant, stronger than seco and gentler than ligero, and of intermediate thickness. Excelling in the balance of strength and aroma, they soften the ligero's power while adding complexity and depth, and burn more evenly than ligero.",
  "Leaves from the middle to lower part of the plant. Seco means 'dry'. Thin, delicate and pale in colour, mild and aromatic, this is the backbone leaf that brings fragrance and good combustion to the filler.",
  "Leaves from the lowest part of the plant, the position with the least sunlight. The thinnest and lightest of the four filler grades. Nearly without flavour but an excellent burner, it plays the vital role of keeping the cigar alight and burning.",
  "The tiny, high-oil leaves that grow only rarely above the ligero, near the crown of the plant. Only about two can be taken from a plant; used in small amounts in limited blends such as Cohiba Behike, they give strong concentration.",
  "The tobacco harvest, or the harvest year. Used in vintage designations, and to emphasise a cigar made from the leaf of a single harvest year. Leaf quality varies from year to year with the weather.",
  "The stage in which the leaves are stacked and left to ferment naturally in their own heat and moisture, drawing off resin, ammonia, harshness and excess nicotine and rounding the flavour. The piles are rebuilt at intervals to control temperature. Fermentation is the heart of cigar making, and the number of rounds and the temperatures vary with leaf type and purpose.",
  "A pile of tobacco leaves, stacked in order on a scale of hundreds of kilograms, for fermentation; also called a burro. It is watched so the interior does not overheat, and rebuilt with the outer and inner leaves exchanged so the fermentation runs evenly.",
  "A square bale of fermented leaf wrapped in the bark of the royal palm. Tercio means 'a third; a bale'. The palm bark holds humidity while letting the leaf breathe, a traditional technique that lets it go on maturing in its own oils. The leaf rests in this state before being sent to the factory.",
  "Long-aged leaf. Añejo means 'old, aged'. The edges come off and it turns mellow, with a deep, settled flavour, and it is chosen as the raw material of limited editions and the Reserva lines.",
  "A group of Cuban-native tobacco varieties whose name means 'native, home-grown'. Used mainly for filler and binder, and some wrapper. The original strain is vulnerable to disease, and improved strains such as Criollo 98 are now widespread.",
  "A variety originally developed in Cuba for wrapper, deriving from the El Corojo plantation. Noted for spice and fragrance but vulnerable to disease, it was replaced in Cuba by improved strains. Today it is grown, and continues to evolve, in Honduras and elsewhere.",
  "The general term for a cigar's shape and size, defined by length (usually in inches or mm) and ring gauge (girth). There are two kinds of designation: the factory name (de galera) and the retail name (de salida).",
  "The measure of a cigar's diameter in units of 1/64 inch. Ring 50, for example, is 50/64 inch, about 19.8 mm. The thicker the cigar, the more smoke, the gentler the burn and the longer the smoking time tend to be. Cepo derives from a sorting board pierced with holes.",
  "The general term for straight cylinders with a closed head and an open foot. Parejo means 'even, uniform'. Most of the standard shapes belong here — Corona, Robusto, Churchill — making it the most common category.",
  "The general term for shaped, non-cylindrical cigars — tapered, swollen or slender at both ends — including the Pirámide, Torpedo, Perfecto, Culebra and Diadema. They demand high skill to roll, and the changing cross-section concentrates the smoke.",
  "A cone shape, pointed at the head and widening continuously towards the foot. Its appeal is concentrated smoke and a generous unfolding of flavour that begins at the broad foot. The exemplar of the figurado.",
  "A shape with a pointed head and a thick body. It resembles the Pirámide, but strictly the body is more or less straight with only the head tapering, and the foot is sometimes closed — the definition has some latitude. The pointed head lets the smoker adjust the cut surface.",
  "A shortish shape whose pointed tip carries a slight rounding. Belicoso means 'bellicose'. Originally the term denoted a small pyramid. In Cuba often ring 52 and around 125 mm long.",
  "A spindle shape, slender at both ends and swelling in the middle, with both head and foot closed. As the lighting end too is slender, lighting takes some ingenuity, but there is the fascination of a flavour that changes as the burn progresses.",
  "A large figurado tapering at both ends. Diadema means 'diadem, crown'. Often over 8 inches long, a stately shape whose thick middle section gives a long smoking time and changes of flavour.",
  "A curious cigar of three panetelas twisted together and bound with string into a snake-like form. Culebra means 'snake'. One account has it that rollers once bundled three together to take home. To smoke, the cigars are untwisted and smoked one at a time.",
  "A large perfecto type, very thick and slendering to a close at both ends. Often over 7 inches with a ring gauge around 57, a shape of real presence offering a long smoke and a rich unfolding of flavour.",
  "A slim, long parejo shape. Slender at around ring 38, it brings out a light, delicate smoke and the aromas that come from the wrapper. The smoking time is comparatively short.",
  "A classic slim shape (around ring 38), about 192 mm long, with a flag (pigtail) at the head. Lancero means 'lancer'. Known as Cohiba's signature size; its high proportion of wrapper makes it aromatic.",
  "A thick, short shape of about 5 inches (124 mm) by around ring 50. Because it delivers the whole picture of a cigar in a short sitting, it is held to be one of the most popular sizes in the world. Cuban factory designations include Robustos.",
  "A large parejo of about 7 inches (178 mm) by around ring 47, named after the British prime minister Winston Churchill, famous as a devotee. It offers a long smoking time and an unfolding of flavour. The Cuban factory name is Julieta (Julieta No.2).",
  "The classic standard size of about 5.5–6 inches (142 mm) by around ring 42. With its fine balance of length and girth, it has served as the benchmark of judgement, held to express the intent of a blend most faithfully. Corona means 'crown'.",
  "A large shape nearly double the Corona: about 7.6 inches (194 mm) by around ring 49. It affords a long, leisurely smoke and layered changes of flavour.",
  "A short Corona of about 5.1 inches (129 mm) by around ring 42. Petit is French for 'small'. An easy smoke of some 30–40 minutes, with the concentrated taste of the Corona's girth.",
  "A form of packaging — and the cigar so packed — in which each cigar is housed singly in a tube of aluminium, glass or wood. Tubo means 'tube'. Convenient to carry and a guard against drying out, but held unsuited to long ageing.",
  "The thin transparent film that wraps cigars singly. It limits damage and humidity loss in transport and display, and keeps fingers off the cigar in the shop. Being porous it lets humidity through, and some devotees age their cigars still wrapped.",
  "The ease with which air and smoke come through on a puff. Tiro means 'pull'. Too loose, and the cigar smokes hot and harsh and burns fast; too tight, and it is hard to draw and thin in taste. Moderate resistance is the ideal, and the packing of the bunch largely decides it.",
  "The state in which over-packed filler or a knot of leaf makes the draw extremely heavy, or stops it altogether. Tapón means 'stopper'. Remedies to try include re-cutting, massaging gently to loosen it, or piercing it through with a purpose-made draw tool (poker).",
  "The technique of blowing gently out through the cigar, without drawing, to expel built-up heat, harshness and excess smoke. Used when bitterness appears in the latter half of the smoke, or before correcting an uneven burn.",
  "The technique of holding the smoke in the mouth and letting it pass quietly out through the nose, tasting the aroma by the retronasal passage. Held to be the advanced smoker's pleasure, perceiving most richly the delicate aromatics of wrapper and blend.",
  "The mode of smell perceived by the route from the mouth, past the throat, to the back of the nasal cavity. Paired with the orthonasal sense (sniffing at the nostrils), it carries the greater part of 'flavour' as bound up with taste. The retrohale puts this perception to use.",
  "The thickness, weight and mouth-filling substance of the taste. Cuerpo means 'body'. Expressed as light, medium or full-bodied. A concept separate from nicotine strength: a cigar can be rich in taste yet not strong.",
  "The physiological power deriving chiefly from nicotine. Fortaleza means 'strength; fortress'. Expressed as mild, medium or full. Too strong, and the unaccustomed smoker suffers nicotine sickness. The proportion of ligero leaf tells here.",
  "The lingering flavour left in mouth and throat after a puff, and its length and quality. Retrogusto means 'aftertaste'. A long, pleasant finish is taken as the mark of high quality; a short, unpleasant one is counted a fault.",
  "The individual elements of flavour and aroma that can be perceived — leather, pepper, cedar, cacao, coffee, nuts, hay and so on. On the model of wine language, they are systematised in flavour wheels.",
  "A positive term for the complex aged aroma suggesting a barn, hay and manure. It appears in well-rested cigars and is taken as the mark of complex, pleasing maturity. It is distinguished from the immature smell of ammonia.",
  "The sharp, pungent smell that remains in young cigars whose fermentation and ageing are incomplete. Taken as a sign that more rest is needed, it fades with a spell of ageing in the humidor.",
  "The state in which flavour, strength, aroma, sweetness, bitterness and the rest are in harmony, with none protruding. Equilibrio means 'equilibrium'. Held to be an essential condition of a fine cigar.",
  "The depth by which varied flavours succeed one another as the smoke progresses. A cigar that changes its expression through opening, middle and close, rather than staying monotonous, is highly rated.",
  "The way the fire advances and the evenness of the burn line. A straight, even line is the ideal. Canoeing and tunnelling are counted as faults; the blend, the rolling and the storage condition all tell.",
  "Uneven combustion in which one side of the cigar burns faster and the burn line slants like a canoe. Causes include uneven humidity in the wrapper, wind and lopsided lighting. It is corrected by purging, or by relighting the slower-burning side.",
  "A burn fault in which the filler burns faster than the wrapper, leaving a tunnel-like hollow at the centre. Attributed to over-drying or an uneven bunch, it is corrected by toasting the wrapper evenly again.",
  "What remains after combustion. A firm ash, white to greyish-white, well held together and long retained, is taken as a sign of good leaf (minerals from the soil and long filler) and careful rolling. A black, crumbly ash can indicate trouble with quality or an unsettled burn.",
  "The first third from lighting. Tercio means 'a third'. The wrapper's aroma rises and forms the introduction to the taste. Reviews describe the changes of flavour by these divisions.",
  "The middle third of the smoke. In many cigars this is where the blend comes into its own — the division in which complexity and body are held to be at their fullest.",
  "The last third. Heat builds, and nicotine and strength tend to rise — it can become the taste's summit or its harshest stretch. It is smoked while letting the heat escape with a purge.",
  "The small bumps and graininess seen on the surface of a wrapper. They are said to form as oil-filled pores dry, and are common on sun-grown leaf. Some regard them as one facet of quality and character.",
  "The state of a wrapper carrying a lustrous sheen of oils. Aceite means 'oil'. Taken as an index of good storage and of the leaf's ripeness and quality, it is rated above a dry, matt state.",
  "The dedicated box, room or cabinet in which cigars are kept at proper humidity. The lining is usually Spanish cedar (cedro), which works to hold humidity, lend aroma and suppress insect damage. Fitted with a humidifier and hygrometer, it is kept at roughly 65–70% RH and 16–18°C.",
  "The wood used to line humidors, divide boxes and wrap single cigars (properly Spanish cedar, a tree of the family Meliaceae). It holds humidity gently, imparts a cedary note to the cigars and tends to keep insects away. Thin slips of it also serve as cedar spills for lighting.",
  "The preparation of a new humidor by charging its wood with moisture and settling the internal climate. To stop dry cedar robbing the cigars of their moisture, the wood is moistened over several days to weeks with distilled water or conditioning packs.",
  "Keeping several cigars in the same box so that their aromas settle into one another and harmonise. It is also the reverse face of the view that different marques should be stored apart, since their scents transfer.",
  "Letting newly acquired cigars settle in the humidor for a spell. Reposo means 'rest'. It rights the disturbance caused by the vibration and humidity swings of transport and restores the true flavour.",
  "Deepening and mellowing a cigar's flavour through long storage in a proper environment. The sharp edges of youth are said to come off, and the several leaves to settle into greater unity. Some devotees rest cigars for years, even beyond a decade.",
  "The storeroom within the factory where finished cigars rest at about 16–18°C and 65–70% humidity, shedding the excess moisture absorbed in manufacture and recovering from the 'fatigue of production'. Escaparate means 'display case; store'. A vital step before dispatch.",
  "The proportion of the air's moisture relative to saturation. The storage guide is roughly 65–70% RH. Too high invites mould and poor combustion; too low, drying, cracking and degraded taste. Since it moves with temperature, both must be managed together.",
  "A conditioning pack that holds the chamber at its set humidity by absorbing and releasing moisture in both directions. Boveda is the leading trade mark. Disposable and easy to manage, they are in wide use.",
  "A white to greyish-white powder that surfaces on the wrapper as the leaf's oils and resins crystallise during ageing. Plume means 'feather'. It falls away at a brush of the finger, is benign, and is taken as proof of maturity. It is distinguished from mould (moho), which is blue-green and spreads like cotton wool.",
  "The blue-green to black mould that arises in over-humid conditions. Moho means 'mould'. Unlike plume it is harmful, carries a smell, and spreads to the surrounding cigars. On discovery, isolate and remove it, and bring the chamber's humidity down.",
  "The general name for Cuba's premium handmade cigars, deriving from the capital Havana (La Habana). The state firm Habanos S.A. directs worldwide sales, and the name is protected as a denomination of origin (D.O.P.). A concept distinct from Habano as the name of a wrapper varietal.",
  "Short for Denominación de Origen Protegida. A system that protects products made in a particular region by particular methods, applied to Habanos and to leaf such as Vuelta Abajo. The highest classification, guaranteeing the authenticity of origin and quality.",
  "The inscription branded on the box. The highest handmade designation, certifying long filler and rolling by hand through every stage with no machine assistance. Ranked above plain Hecho a mano (handmade).",
  "An inscription stating explicitly that long filler is used, often appearing alongside Totalmente a mano. It guarantees a premium product using whole leaves rather than chopped filler.",
  "The branded inscription on the box base signifying Cuban manufacture. Proof of origin, used together with the other inscriptions and the Cuban Government warranty seal in judging authenticity.",
  "An inscription indicating handwork. It may, however, cover short filler or machine-assisted bunching, and is not as strict as Totalmente a mano.",
  "The Habanos limited series begun in 2000. Made in limited yearly quantities from leaf aged two years or more, with notably dark wrappers. The band and box carry the year of release. Abbreviated EL.",
  "Habanos produced in limited runs for the distributor of a particular country or region. An existing marque is given a size not in its released range, exclusive to the region, with a dedicated secondary band. Begun in 2005.",
  "A special edition made from the leaf of a single harvest year, aged three years or more. Marked by a silver band on black, with the harvest year (cosecha) stated. Produced in limited numbers.",
  "The highest limited edition, standing above the Reserva: choicest leaf of a single harvest year, aged five years or more. A gold band on black; produced only in very small numbers.",
  "The Habanos line, begun in 2015, of finished cigars aged in their boxes in the factory's conditions for five years or more before release. In resting the finished product it differs from the Reserva (which ages the leaf), and it carries a dedicated band.",
  "The hobby of collecting and studying cigar bands (anillas) and vitola-related paper. Collectors are called vitolphilists. Historic bands sometimes trade at high prices.",
  "The packing unit of cigars bundled with ribbon. Rueda means 'wheel' — normally 50 cigars; the media rueda is half that, 25. A traditional bundling managed from the factory by galera, often housed in an SLB (slide-lid box).",
  "A wooden box opened by sliding its lid, created in the early twentieth century for select 'cabinet-quality' Habanos bound for Britain. The cigars inside are round-bodied and bundled with a silk ribbon. Nearly synonymous with the cabinet selection.",
  "Packaging of 25 or 50 cigars loosely packed in ribbon-tied bundles in a slide-lid wooden box. Cabinet means 'cupboard'. With air to spare between the cigars it is considered suited to ageing, and is favoured by devotees who lay cigars down. The boxes are often tall.",
  "The most common cigar box: wood or stout card covered in decorated paper bearing logos, crests and seals, its box-pressed cigars packed without gaps. The semi boîte (semi-dress) is its simplified version.",
  "A dedicated box with rounded sides holding 25 cigars in three tiers — eight above, nine in the middle, eight below — the numbers deriving from the count in each tier. Held to be a traditional and handsome premium form of packing.",
  "Aficionado-forum shorthand for Cuban Cigar — a cigar of Cuban origin. Used as a pair with NC (non-Cuban).",
  "Aficionado-forum shorthand. Non-Cuban: a cigar of origins other than Cuba (Dominican Republic, Nicaragua, Honduras and so on).",
  "An acronym for 'Island South of Miami'. Against the background of the US embargo on Cuba, it arose on the forums as an oblique code word for Cuba.",
  "Short for 'Brother of the Leaf'. An affectionate term for a fellow male cigar lover.",
  "Short for 'Sister of the Leaf'. The term for a female cigar lover, forming a pair with BOTL.",
  "A gathering at which cigar lovers meet to smoke together and socialise. Held at lounges and events, with news exchanged and cigars traded as well.",
  "Smoking a cigar down to the very last stub, until it warms the fingers — and that short stub itself. The better the cigar, the longer the taste holds to the end. A brand of the same name (Nub) also exists.",
  "Short for 'Right Off The Truck': smoking a cigar the moment it arrives, without a rest. Since the true taste rarely shows straight after transport, the term is used in contexts recommending a rest instead.",
  "The cutting method that opens a round hole in the head to make the mouth. Because it does not cut the cap away at large, it resists unravelling and concentrates the smoke, but it suits thick ring gauges poorly. A dedicated punch cutter is used.",
  "The most common cut, slicing the cap off flat in a single straight line. Cortador means 'cutting tool'. There are single- and double-bladed versions, and the wide opening gives a generous draw. Cut too deep and the wrapper unravels, so take care.",
  "The method that cuts a V-shaped (wedge) groove into the head; also called a cat's eye. Keeping much of the cap while preserving the area of the opening, it resists crumbling, and the smoke is said to gather readily at the centre of the tongue.",
  "A second, decorative band wrapped at the foot (lighting) end. Used on limited and commemorative editions and removed before lighting, it serves ornament and identification alike.",
  "A decorative finish winding wrappers of two shades (say, claro and maduro) round the cigar in alternating spirals, named for its likeness to the barber's pole outside a barber's shop. It offers the flavours of two wrappers at once.",
  "The palest wrapper, yellow-green to blue-green, obtained by picking the leaf early and drying it rapidly at high heat so that the chlorophyll is retained. Also called double claro. It once enjoyed great popularity on the American market. A mild, distinctive flavour like young grass.",
  "A pale tan to golden-brown wrapper shade. Claro means 'light, clear'. Mostly shade-grown, it tends to show a smooth, mild, creamy smoke. Connecticut Shade is the classic example.",
  "A light-brown, natural-coloured wrapper — also simply 'natural' — mostly sun-grown. Also called EMS (English Market Selection), a name said to derive from the Cuban practice of selecting whether leaf was of sufficient quality for the overseas market.",
  "A reddish middle shade (medium brown) of wrapper. Colorado means 'coloured, reddish'. It often shows a rich, full flavour, and is seen on sun-grown or well-matured leaf.",
  "A deep brown between colorado and maduro. Common on Honduran and similar marques, it is placed as the middle shade lending deep, full-bodied flavour.",
  "A wrapper brought to deep brown by long fermentation at high temperature. Maduro means 'ripe'. Sugars are drawn out, giving sweetness, richness and notes of chocolate and coffee. The colour is set by the leaf's origin, variety and degree of fermentation, and does not necessarily track strength.",
  "The darkest wrapper shade, close to black. Oscuro means 'dark'. Obtained by the longest, hottest fermentation, or from thick sun-grown leaf, it shows a dense, sweet and often powerful flavour.",
  "Nausea, dizziness, cold sweats and palpitations from an excess of nicotine, colloquially called 'going green'. It strikes most readily when a strong cigar is smoked fast on an empty stomach, and is eased by taking sugar and resting.",
  "Fermenting the leaf in two separate rounds to draw off still more resin, ammonia and harshness and deepen the flavour. It is especially important in the making of dark wrappers and maduros.",
  "French for 'natural box': a premium cigar box of plain Spanish cedar with a hinged lid, left uncovered by decorated paper. It lets the wood's own character show, and imparts the cedar note to the cigars within."
  ];
  if (W.lexicon.length === LEX.length) W.lexicon.forEach(function (o, i) { o.desc = LEX[i]; });
})();
